<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  Windows application manifest.

  RULE: NEVER PUT A DOUBLE HYPHEN INSIDE AN XML COMMENT.

  This looks like a trivial style rule. It is not. XML forbids the two-hyphen
  sequence inside comments, and mt.exe (which link.exe invokes to process
  /MANIFESTINPUT) reports the resulting parse error in a way that points
  nowhere useful:

      app.manifest : general error c1010070: Failed to load and parse the
                     manifest. Windows cannot process the XML data.
      LINK : fatal error LNK1327: error during running mt.exe

  Nothing in that message mentions comments. One double hyphen buried deep in
  a long comment is enough to break the entire link step. Measured with a 2x2
  test against mt.exe directly:

      ascii,     no double hyphen   -> OK
      ascii,     double hyphen      -> FAIL
      chinese,   no double hyphen   -> OK     (encoding is NOT the problem)
      chinese,   double hyphen      -> FAIL

  So UTF-8 without BOM is fine here. The double hyphen is the actual killer.
  This file is kept ASCII-only anyway, purely to keep Windows tooling simple.

  ===========================================================================
  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL
  ===========================================================================

  Without it, `cargo test` on Windows cannot even start: the process dies
  immediately with 0xc0000139 STATUS_ENTRYPOINT_NOT_FOUND and no Rust level
  error at all. Unit test hosts and example binaries crash identically, so it
  is not a test harness problem.

  Root cause: the Tauri WebView runtime calls comctl32.dll!TaskDialogIndirect.
  That export exists only in comctl32 v6, and v6 is loaded only when the
  program ships a manifest declaring a dependency on
  Microsoft.Windows.Common-Controls 6.0.0.0. Otherwise the loader falls back
  to System32\comctl32.dll (v5.82), which lacks the export.

  Why tauri-build is not enough: it does emit this manifest, but through
  embed-resource, which prints `cargo:rustc-link-arg-bins` (that applies only
  to the bin target). Test hosts and examples never receive it.

  So build.rs disables that half (WindowsAttributes::new_without_app_manifest)
  and injects this file itself via the target agnostic pair
  `cargo:rustc-link-arg=/MANIFEST:EMBED` and
  `cargo:rustc-link-arg=/MANIFESTINPUT:<absolute path>`.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <dependency>
    <dependentAssembly>
      <assemblyIdentity
        type="win32"
        name="Microsoft.Windows.Common-Controls"
        version="6.0.0.0"
        processorArchitecture="*"
        publicKeyToken="6595b64144ccf1df"
        language="*"
      />
    </dependentAssembly>
  </dependency>

  <!-- Declare per monitor DPI awareness, otherwise the window is bitmap scaled
       and looks blurry on high DPI displays. -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
      <longPathAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">true</longPathAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
